00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
when you think about  the current environment obviously the  federal reserve's been  increasing interest rates and they've  been  conducting quantitative tightening or at  least saying that they're going to do it  how do you think about asset prices  across the spectrum in relation to that  should we just see correlations go  towards one and all asset prices sell  off or should we expect to have some  sort of decoupling in this type of  environment  that's such a great question i think  it's on everyone's mind right now as  kind of the macro framework and monetary  policy kind of dominates the market  narrative right now  and from my perspective  one of the things that you learn in kind  of like entry-level finance and  economics courses is that yields and  asset prices have an inverse correlation  all else being equal and so as we're in  an interest rate environment where  the fed is tightening monetary policy  and yields on treasuries are are moving  higher right um  in that environment we've seen asset  prices across the board whether we're  looking at  stocks or bitcoin and crypto or even  treasuries in the bond market everything  is selling off in this environment and  so from my perspective so long as we  remain in this environment asset prices  are going to have headwinds  um  to to rise and benefit in this  environment right so until we really see  a formal shift  in  basically the yield environment am i  expecting to see a bull market  yeah when you start to think about um  kind of the transition from let's call  the bear market we're in now pretty much  across asset classes into a bull market  is it just when does the fed let their  foot off the gas pedal or is there other  things that you would be looking for  i think there's um some other things  right like the fed is clearly behind the  curve in this environment and so if we  start to see inflation data formally  roll over and kind of sustain that  rollover then i think that could be a a  general foreshadowing of a reversal in  the monetary policy environment but um  if we look generally speaking at  stock market and crypto relative to  monetary policy we can kind of get some  clues as to how these assets have  behaved in the past relative to monetary  policy and so if we look at um when  bitcoin and the stock market bottomed in  2018  it was in december of 2018 when the fed  formally paused their rate hikes and so  we had a formal shift in the monetary  policy outlook and environment when did  asset prices bottom in 2020 including  crypto right is the fed came out and  formally said we are here to provide  infinite support to the monetary and  financial system and asset prices ripped  higher when did stocks and bitcoin and  crypto broadly peak in november of 2021  when the fed announced that they were  tapering so  it's a it's a super simple and kind of  like crude metric for where asset prices  are going to go going forward but it's  been super accurate in this kind of like  monetary wonderland that we're in right  yeah when we start to look at certain  parts of the market uh there's aspects  that i know you've been paying attention  to that we just haven't talked about  that much on this show so i'd love to  kind of go through this uh the first is  this idea of loans and leases uh and  you've identified that it's one  expanding at a rapid pace um but also  there's this relationship with inflation  so maybe explain a little bit  about this loans and leases and how  people should think about uh this as  part of the financial system sure so  i spent two years working on the  commercial banking side as a portfolio  analyst so i got really familiar with  looking at this kind of data overall  and i've been out of that field now for  almost two years so nothing i'm about to  say is indicative of like my former  employer or anything like that right but  um  essentially when i look at loans and  leases it i think it's very important  because as we think about the  inflationary dynamics  obviously the fed came out and they  printed all of this money and operated  qe through like crazy right for 24  months plus  and  obviously the monetary base in m2  expanded during that period but until we  see that money really come into the  actual real economy and leave the  financial and banking system  i think that's going to bring more  inflationary pressures in so in the  upper bound of this chart we're looking  at the nominal value of total loans and  leases that have been issued by  commercial banks in the united states  and it just hit an all-time high in  early june at roughly 11.3 trillion  dollars  in the lower bound of this chart what  i've done is i've actually denominated  loans and leases and i've divided it by  m2 money supply so this gives us  somewhat of an indication of credit  creation  relative to the monetary base and if we  look over time basically since 2000 we  get these distinct kind of rolling overs  at the tops and curling back up during  an uptrend and it appears as though for  the past six to seven months loans and  leases relative to the monetary base are  starting to increase again and i think  that's one of the main reasons why we've  continued to see inflationary pressures  in 2022.  so when you see this basically people  are just borrowing what was cheap  capital now becoming more expensive  and they're using other people's money  essentially to go ahead and buy assets  to consume do whatever uh they're doing  from a spending standpoint and that  ultimately influx of kind of like net  new capitals driving up asset prices and  also consumables  and kind of increasing demand and  therefore until we start to see relief  here then we shouldn't expect inflation  to come down materially and part of the  fed's actions to increase the cost of  capital is to drastically reduce the  demand for these loans and leases and  therefore bring inflation down i think  that's a that's a fair assessment pomp  and  um you know this this data here loans  and leases includes consumer loans it  includes loans secured by real estate it  includes commercial and industrial loans  so this is really a full picture of the  total credit market if you will in terms  of the business and consumer demand for  new loans  got it and so when we start to see um  this number come down how quickly would  you expect it to come down is it  something where uh okay now that  everyone realizes the fed's serious at a  75 basis point hike and we should expect  to see this data like fall off a cliff  or is it something that's more gradual  and kind of takes time for people to  recognize the market is changing the  cost of capital is changing and  therefore uh i was going to get a loan  in september but actually i'm not going  to do that and so that would take a  couple of months before it got kind of  quote unquote priced into the market or  revealed in this data  sure so uh one main point on this that  i'd like to say is that i expect to see  loans and leases continue to expand even  if we go into a recessionary environment  because even in that environment  businesses are going to need capital to  operate  right and so  the one thing that i'm really going to  be paying attention to is the rate of  change and the growth rate of loans and  leases so if we look at um the  year-over-year growth in loans and  leases it's about nine percent and  what's the inflation rate right now  measured by the cpi it's eight point six  percent and there's no rule or law or  theory that says that these should grow  in line with each other um  but i think if we want to see inflation  data materially roll over we're going to  need to see a  deceleration in the growth rate of loans  and leases i think that's the way that i  would put it  and on a year-to-date basis loans and  loans and leases are up almost four and  a half five percent so again we're  seeing ample demand from businesses to  acquire new debt and expand production  whatever the case is i'm not sure  exactly what they're using the capital  for per se  it could be to buy back their own stock  or  increase their dividend right i'm not  sure  um  but even if we look at sub sectors of  loans and leases and we look at  something like commercial and industrial  loans which i really view as something  like capital expenditures or  growth in the production  something of that nature  that number right now is about 2.6  trillion dollars and so that's also  growing at a pretty solid rate much  slower than overall loans and leases but  still increasing so that to me just  implies that credit creation is on the  rise

